+
+# Personal API tokens for the JSON posts API
+
+## Context
+
+Authors publish from scripts (cron jobs posting release notes, CI steps
+listing posts) by replaying login cookies against the HTML pages, which
+breaks whenever a page changes. They need a JSON API with credentials they
+create and revoke themselves, and ops needs a cap on how hard any one
+script can hit the site.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Tokens are the framework's bearer tokens: `AuthProvider` calls
+  `auth.useTokens()` with a `DatabaseApiTokenStore` over the `api_tokens`
+  table, shared through `app/Auth/ApiTokenStore.ts` so the guard and the
+  controller use one store. Any request carrying `Authorization: Bearer`
+  authenticates through that guard as the token's owner.
+- The table stores only a hash of the secret half of the `{id}|{secret}`
+  token. The plain text is returned once, by `POST /profile/tokens`, and
+  never again; the profile page lists tokens by name only.
+- `POST /profile/tokens` and `DELETE /profile/tokens/:id` live behind the
+  session `auth` alias. Revocation is scoped to the owner, so another
+  user's token id answers 404 and stays valid.
+- `GET /api/posts` and `POST /api/posts` sit behind two aliases:
+  `auth.api` (`requireAuthenticated()` with no redirect, so a guest gets a
+  JSON 401) and `throttle.api`, a 60-per-minute rate limit keyed by token
+  id, not by user or IP. A limited request answers 429 with `Retry-After`.
+- The API reuses `PostPayloadSchema` and `PostResource`, so validation and
+  the wire shape match the web form. `authorId` always comes from the
+  token's owner via `forceCreate`, as the web controller already does.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A leaked database dump yields no usable credentials, and a lost token
+  cannot be recovered, only replaced.
+- Token scopes, expiry, paging, and editing or deleting posts over the API
+  are not covered; `abilities` is stored as `['*']` for every token so
+  scopes can be added without a migration.
+- The rate limit store is in-memory, so the cap is per process. A
+  multi-process deployment needs a shared `RateLimitStore`.
